# Predictions for dp approach
- DP will find the optimal makespan for instances it can finish.
- More jobs will generally increase runtime and memory use.
- More machines will generally increase the number of load states.
- Repeated job times should produce fewer distinct states than varied times.
- Early stopping should help when the optimum matches the lower bound.
- DP should use more time and memory than LPT, with equal or better makespans.
- DP should be practical mainly for smaller instances.
- Inputs with the same job and machine counts may have very different runtimes.
- Memoisation should reduce repeated computation compared with uncached recursion.
- Sorting load tuples should reduce cached states compared with keeping machine order.
- Skipping equal-load machines should reduce redundant cache lookups.
- Early stopping should offer no benefit when the optimum exceeds the lower bound.
- Changing job order may affect runtime but should preserve the optimal makespan.
- Multiplying all job times by the same positive integer should preserve the state count.

